.TH AETHER-ACK 1 "October 2026" "Aether 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
aether-ack \- acknowledge and suppress operational triage anomalies
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aether ack
\fIPATH\fR
[\fB\-\-reason\fR \fITEXT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBack\fR command records an administrator acknowledgment for an inspected file that triggered a benign anomaly (SUSPICIOUS warning). It computes the SHA-256 hash of the target object and appends an entry to the acknowledgment registry.
.PP
Subsequent scans encountering the exact same file with matching cryptographic hash and path will categorize the object as an acknowledged baseline finding rather than raising repeated alerts.
.SH SECURITY CONSTRAINTS
.TP
.B Non-Ackable Alerts
Confirmed threats (\fBMALICIOUS\fR tier, score $\ge$ 85, such as reverse shells or ransomware lockers) \fBCANNOT\fR be acknowledged. The engine refuses to suppress confirmed malware indicators.
.TP
.B Hash Invalidation
If an acknowledged file is modified on disk, its cryptographic hash changes, immediately invalidating the acknowledgment and triggering fresh inspection.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/aether/acknowledged.yaml
System-wide acknowledgment registry (used in privileged root mode).
.TP
.I ~/.config/aether/acknowledged.yaml
Per-user acknowledgment registry (used in unprivileged mode).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBaether\fR(1), \fBaether-scan\fR(1), \fBaether-rules\fR(1)
